   "excerpt": "Advent is not a countdown to presents. It is four weeks of teaching your children to wait in the dark for a light that is coming, which is the whole shape of the Christian life.",
   "topic": "family",
   "readTime": "6 min read",
-  "body": "December arrives and the culture has one instruction for your family: consume, and hurry while you do it. The lights go up, the lists go out, and by the time the actual day arrives, your children are exhausted by a holiday they were promised would be magic. There is an older way, and it runs in the opposite direction.\n\nAdvent is the church's long practice of waiting. The word means coming, and the four weeks before Christmas were set apart not to celebrate early but to wait well, to feel the ache of a world that is not yet right and a King who has not yet come. It is the one season that teaches children something the rest of the year cannot. How to want the right thing, and how to wait for it.\n\n## Why waiting is the lesson\n\nWe have built a world with no waiting in it. Every want is answered before it has time to deepen. A child raised on instant everything does not know how to long for anything, and a person who cannot long cannot hope. Hope is desire stretched across time. Advent is training in hope.\n\nThe whole of Israel's story was a waiting. Centuries of prophets promising a deliverer who did not come, generations living and dying in the in-between, the long silence between Malachi and Matthew. When Jesus finally came, he came to people who had almost stopped expecting him. Advent puts your family inside that waiting on purpose, so that Christmas lands not as a scheduled event but as an answer to a longing you actually felt.\n\n## A simple Advent practice\n\nGet a wreath with four candles, or just four candles in a row. One for each Sunday of December. The point is the growing light. You light one the first week, two the second, and so on, so that the room gets brighter as the day draws near. Children feel this in their bodies before they understand it in their heads. The dark is real, and the light is winning.\n\nEach evening, or each Sunday if every evening is too much, light the candles and read a short passage. Walk the prophecies of the coming King. Isaiah's child who is born to us. Micah's ruler from little Bethlehem. The angel's word to Mary. Read them in order across the weeks so the story builds toward the manger.\n\nThen say one sentence and pray. \"We are waiting for Jesus, like Israel waited, and he always comes.\" Keep it short. The candles do most of the teaching.\n\n## Resist the urge to rush to Christmas\n\nThe hardest part of Advent is not doing it. It is not skipping to the end. Everything around you is already singing about the birth, already opened, already finished. Advent asks your family to stay in the waiting a little longer than is comfortable, to let the longing build before the relief comes.\n\nThis is countercultural and it will feel strange, and your children may complain that other families are further along. Let them. You are teaching them that the best things are worth waiting for, and that the waiting itself is part of the gift. A child who learns this in December has learned something that will steady them in every dark season of their life, when the light is promised but not yet visible.\n\nFamilies rush Advent. They light all the candles early because the waiting feels pointless and the kids are impatient. It is a mistake, and most of us know it even as we make it. The waiting is the point we are throwing away.\n\n## Let the longing be honest\n\nAdvent is allowed to be heavy. It names a world that is broken and a rescue that is not finished. If your family has known loss, Advent does not paper over it. It says the King is coming to set all of it right, and he has not finished yet, and we wait. Children can hold this. They live closer to longing than adults do. Give them words for it.\n\n## This week\n\nBefore the first Sunday of December, set four candles where your family eats. On that Sunday, light one, read Isaiah 9, and say a sentence about waiting for the King. Each week add a candle and read the next prophecy. Hold the line. Do not light them all early. Let the room grow brighter as Christmas approaches, and let your children feel, in their own small bodies, the ache of waiting for a light that is surely coming.\n\nThen on Christmas morning, light them all. They will understand, without being told, what the brightness means."
+  "body": "December arrives and the culture has one instruction for your family: consume, and hurry while you do it. The lights go up, the lists go out, and by the time the actual day arrives, your children are exhausted by a holiday they were promised would be magic. There is an older way, and it runs in the opposite direction.\n\nAdvent is the church's long practice of waiting. The word means coming, and the four weeks before Christmas were set apart not to celebrate early but to wait well, to feel the ache of a world that is not yet right and a King who has not yet come. It is the one season that teaches children something the rest of the year cannot. How to want the right thing, and how to wait for it.\n\n## Why waiting is the lesson\n\nWe have built a world with no waiting in it. Every want is answered before it has time to deepen. A child raised on instant everything does not know how to long for anything, and a person who cannot long cannot hope. Hope is desire stretched across time. Advent is training in hope.\n\nThe whole of Israel's story was a waiting. Centuries of prophets promising a deliverer who did not come, generations living and dying in the in-between, the long silence between Malachi and Matthew. When Jesus finally came, he came to people who had almost stopped expecting him. Advent puts your family inside that waiting on purpose, so that Christmas lands not as a scheduled event but as an answer to a longing you actually felt.\n\n## A simple Advent practice\n\nGet a wreath with four candles, or just four candles in a row. One for each Sunday of Advent. The point is the growing light. You light one the first week, two the second, and so on, so that the room gets brighter as the day draws near. Children feel this in their bodies before they understand it in their heads. The dark is real, and the light is winning.\n\nEach evening, or each Sunday if every evening is too much, light the candles and read a short passage. Walk the prophecies of the coming King. Isaiah's child who is born to us. Micah's ruler from little Bethlehem. The angel's word to Mary. Read them in order across the weeks so the story builds toward the manger.\n\nThen say one sentence and pray. \"We are waiting for Jesus, like Israel waited, and he always comes.\" Keep it short. The candles do most of the teaching.\n\n## Resist the urge to rush to Christmas\n\nThe hardest part of Advent is not doing it. It is not skipping to the end. Everything around you is already singing about the birth, already opened, already finished. Advent asks your family to stay in the waiting a little longer than is comfortable, to let the longing build before the relief comes.\n\nThis is countercultural and it will feel strange, and your children may complain that other families are further along. Let them. You are teaching them that the best things are worth waiting for, and that the waiting itself is part of the gift. A child who learns this in December has learned something that will steady them in every dark season of their life, when the light is promised but not yet visible.\n\nFamilies rush Advent. They light all the candles early because the waiting feels pointless and the kids are impatient. It is a mistake, and most of us know it even as we make it. The waiting is the point we are throwing away.\n\n## Let the longing be honest\n\nAdvent is allowed to be heavy. It names a world that is broken and a rescue that is not finished. If your family has known loss, Advent does not paper over it. It says the King is coming to set all of it right, and he has not finished yet, and we wait. Children can hold this. They live closer to longing than adults do. Give them words for it.\n\n## This week\n\nBefore the first Sunday of Advent, set four candles where your family eats. On that Sunday, light one, read Isaiah 9, and say a sentence about waiting for the King. Each week add a candle and read the next prophecy. Hold the line. Do not light them all early. Let the room grow brighter as Christmas approaches, and let your children feel, in their own small bodies, the ache of waiting for a light that is surely coming.\n\nThen on Christmas morning, light them all. They will understand, without being told, what the brightness means."
